maya_sales: Quick kickoff for ACME POC — they want a Node.js sample app that uses streaming (SSE/streaming fetch), supports tool/function calls (search + db), and returns structured JSON that maps to their UI. Also need answers for pricing (streaming), CORS/handshake, and SOC2/KMS notes. Can someone drop a minimal snippet + handshake pattern?

jess_devex: On it. I pushed a minimal example to gist earlier, but here's a compact snippet for server-side Node streaming + parsing chunks. Works well for our Node SDK or plain fetch streaming: 

```js
// server-side streaming read (Node 18+)
const res = await fetch('https://api.redwood.com/v1/streams', {
  method: 'POST',
  headers: { 'Authorization': `Bearer ${token}`, 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
  body: JSON.stringify({ model: 'redwood-stream-small', input: 'Summarize customer tickets' })
});
const reader = res.body.getReader();
const dec = new TextDecoder();
let buf = '';
while(true){
  const { done, value } = await reader.read();
  if(done) break;
  buf += dec.decode(value, { stream: true });
  // messages come as newline-delimited JSON chunks
  let parts = buf.split('\n');
  buf = parts.pop();
  for(const p of parts){
    if(!p.trim()) continue;
    const msg = JSON.parse(p);
    console.log('chunk', msg);
    // handle tool_call / final / partial
  }
}
```

jess_devex: For tool/function calls we emit a structured chunk like:

```json
{"type":"tool_call","tool":"search","args":{"q":"error 500 logs"}}
```

sam_soleng: Quick notes: our Node SDK wraps that and exposes callbacks: `onStreamChunk`, `onToolCall` and `onComplete`. Example: `client.stream({model, input}).on('tool_call', (payload)=>{ /* call your service and send tool result back */})`. We also support sending tool results back via a single POST to `/v1/streams/{stream_id}/tool_result` (server-to-server).

ryan_sre: CORS/handshake detail — do NOT send customer API keys from browser. Recommended approach: customer webapp calls their backend -> backend does a short-lived handshake with Redwood to mint ephemeral session token (10-60s TTL) scoped to that stream+origin. We provide a handshake endpoint: POST /v1/handshake {client_id, allowed_origin} -> returns ephemeral token and stream_id. Browser then connects to your backend SSE endpoint which proxies to Redwood, or you use a signed cookie. If they insist direct-from-browser, explain security tradeoffs.

aditi_sec: Security checklist answers (high level):
- SOC2: yes, available (link to SOC2 report on request).
- Data at rest: encrypted with customer-managed KMS for Private deployments; hosted uses provider-managed keys but we offer Bring-Your-Own-Key for Dedicated/Private.
- Network: VPC peering / Private deployments available for regulated workloads.
- Audit logs: request-level logs + token tracing retained per contract.
- Short-lived creds + RBAC for POCs recommended.

maya_sales: Pricing / cost question from ACME: they want estimate for 10k concurrent streaming connections during peak (each ~30s avg, 150 tokens generated avg). Any ballpark?

sam_soleng: Ballpark math: we charge per-token for input+output. Streaming keepalive/connection cost is negligible unless you use very long-lived connections — we bill small per-minute for reserved concurrency (Dedicated). For hosted: assume 150 output tokens * $0.0004/token = $0.06 per session; 10k concurrent for 30s -> that's 10k * 0.06 = $600 per peak minute in gross output token cost. Add inhaled tokens and safety margin (+~20%). If they require guaranteed throughput consider Dedicated pricing (commit + lower per-token). I'll ping pricing team for an exact quote.

maya_sales: Thanks. ACME also requires responses to strict JSON schema — they want all responses under `items[]` with fields `id,title,confidence`. Any pitfalls?

jess_devex: Two tips: (1) Use our structured output mode and provide an output_schema in the request so the model is constrained. Example: `output_schema: {type:'array', name:'items', items:{id:'string', title:'string', confidence:'number'}}`. We will emit parseable JSON chunks. (2) Add a post-processing validation layer: on tool_call completion the SDK returns the parsed object; validate schema and reject if missing required fields. We also support automatic retries with a different temperature if schema validation fails.

ryan_sre: For production POC, watch for partial/chunked JSON — always buffer until you detect a terminal `type: final` chunk or `\n` row. Our clients expose `onComplete` with final parsed object to simplify this.

